  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The evidence from pmid-23766237 confirms that TERT promoter mutations (specifically C228T) are found in various forms of thyroid cancer, including papillary, follicular, poorly differentiated, and anaplastic thyroid cancers, as well as thyroid cancer cell lines. The mutations are described as being significantly more prevalent in certain subtypes (e.g., TCPTC vs CPTC/FVPTC) and are associated with aggressive features (e.g., higher prevalence in anaplastic and poorly differentiated cancers). This supports the claim that TERT promoter mutations are a diagnostic marker in thyroid cancer, with a positive significance and somatic origin.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-23766237",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 580,
        "quote": "We found the C228T mutation in 0 of 85 (0.0%) benign thyroid tumors, 30 of 257 (11.7%) papillary thyroid cancers (PTC), 9 of 79 (11.4%) follicular thyroid cancers (FTC), 3 of 8 (37.5%) poorly differentiated thyroid cancers (PDTC), 23 of 54 (42.6%) anaplastic thyroid cancers (ATC), and 8 of 12 (66.7%) thyroid cancer cell lines. . . . As shown in Table 3, TERT promoter mutations were significantly more prevalent in the TCPTC samples than in the CPTC and FVPTC samples, 30.8% (4/13) in the former vs 10.3% (25/243) in the latter two (P=0.046, per two-tailed Fisher's exact test)."
      }
    ],
